    "raw": "Title: Jim Cramer on UnitedHealth: “The Stock Deserves to Be Much Higher”\nDate: 2026-04-26 15:19\nURL: https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/jim-cramer-unitedhealth-stock-deserves-151919131.html?.tsrc=rss\n\nAmericans are feeling less confident about their retirement savings. Here are some of the best ways to get started early.\nBMW has long treated the 7 Series as its technological proving ground — where its ambitious ideas first find a home.\nBank of America's 'sleep like a baby' portfolio is having its best year since 1933\nWarsh confirmation set to advance as GOP holdout Tillis backs vote (Bloomberg)\nElon Musk's lawsuit against Sam Altman and OpenAI goes to trial Monday, and the company's future may hang in the balance.\nThe week ahead sees Jerome Powell's penultimate meeting as Fed chair, and five of the Mag 7 will report earnings.\nWith gas prices over $4 a gallon, interest in hybrids and EVs appears to be on the rise among prospective car buyers.\nThe best (and worst) time of year to buy a house\nTrump cancels trip by US negotiators for peace talks with Iran (Financial Times)\nThe traditional notion of where to move in retirement is getting a shakeup. Here's what to know.\nAfter a post-pandemic bust, home buying is picking up in Austin.\nSoftware's comeback bid is fading as chip stocks smash records\nFood prices were already straining Americans’ pocketbooks. The Iran war could make the problem even starker.\nBig Tech's jobs cuts feel like an economic warning\nNvidia leaps to new high, pushing market cap above $5 trillion again\nThe Nasdaq and S&P 500 jumped to record highs to end an earnings-filled week as Intel topped its 2000 peak\nThe Department of Justice is dropping its criminal probe of Federal Reserve Chair Jerome Powell.\nAfter an earnings beat, Intel’s stock is blasting through a dot-com-era ceiling\nNasdaq and S&P 500 rise as Intel stock soars, Dow slips amid Hormuz tensions\nMeta to buy Graviton chips from Amazon as CPU sales soar\nIntel stock soars on earnings beat and strong outlook driven by data center demand\nThe Dow, S&P 500, and Nasdaq retreated as software stocks plunged, while oil surged on Iran war concerns\nMeta cuts thousands of jobs; Microsoft reportedly offers buyouts to 7% of workers\nUS stocks slide as oil rises amid Hormuz standoff, while Tesla slips after earnings\nWhy Tesla investors should love this version of CEO Elon Musk\nTesla shares jumps as earnings, sales top forecasts\nThe S&P 500 and Nasdaq hit fresh records as market rally resumes, with Tesla earnings on deck\nUS stocks rise after Trump extends the US-Iran ceasefire indefinitely\nBest Buy taps insider Jason Bonfig as new CEO, as Corie Barry steps down\nDow, S&P 500, Nasdaq futures rise after Trump extends US-Iran ceasefire\nKevin Warsh just faced a fiery Senate hearing. He still doesn’t have a clear path to becoming Fed chair.\nThe Dow, S&P 500, and Nasdaq slid as US-Iran peace talks were put on hold\nKevin Warsh testifies he never made a deal with Trump to lower interest rates as Fed chair\nFed nominee Kevin Warsh testified that he won't be Trump's \"sock puppet\" in contentious Senate hearing. Follow live.\nKevin Warsh, President Trump's pick to lead the Fed, is set to testify in front of the Senate Banking Committee. Follow along live.\nUS stocks climb with Warsh hearing, Iran talks in focus\nTim Cook is stepping down as Apple CEO. His 15-year run made investors $4 trillion richer.\nApple CEO Tim Cook steps down, John Ternus to take role\nDow slips, S&P 500 and Nasdaq retreat from records with Middle East peace talks at risk\nScott Melker delivers a high-octane, 15-minute crypto briefing every weekday on Yahoo Finance. Check out the first episode\nStocks edged lower as US-Iran tensions resumed, shuttering the Strait of Hormuz\nA longer-term car loan can lower the barrier to entry for buyers — but these loan terms are not without drawbacks.\nThe AI trade has been able to insulate itself from disruptions caused by the Middle East war.\nGas prices may remain at $3 per gallon or more until next year, US Energy Secretary Chris Wright said Sunday. (Bloomberg)\nAs big banks kick off earnings season, strategists say the \"dominant theme\" for investors is shifting back to Big Tech and energy.\nCeasefire talks, record highs for the US stock market, and Tesla earnings will be in focus for investors.\nIn the rarefied world of luxury automobiles, Rolls-Royce just upped the altitude.\nGas prices \"rise like a rocket and fall like a feather.\"  Here's what to know.\nThe million dollar retirement planning question: How long will you live?\nThe software rebound is real, but not every big name is back\nUnitedHealth Group Incorporated (NYSE: UNH ) was among Jim Cramer’s latest stock calls, as he suggested investors balance portfolios with hot and cold stocks . Cramer mentioned that despite the company reporting the “best managed care earnings I’ve seen in ages,” its stock still went down.\nOkay, finally, we’re going to get to the one that you’re going to buy tomorrow, alright, UnitedHealth Group. Here’s a company that just reported its first solid beat and raise quarter in a very long time. Returning CEO, Steve Hemsley, who turned UNH into a juggernaut before he retired in 2017, is back, and he is doing the same thing he did for years the last time he was CEO. Produced the best earnings and biggest upside surprises in the real. They’re not chimerical. They’re not made up. They’re none of that jack stuff.\nYet after all of that, after putting up the best managed care earnings I’ve seen in ages, stock still sells for just 19 times earnings. Boy, this group is hated. The man is just getting started. The stock deserves to be much higher. Okay, here’s a little clue. UNH, we’ll do a little cinéma-vérité here… The UNH is at $354. This stock was at $600 last April, April of 2025. You know why I like these stocks so much? Because if they come down further, you can just keep buying them hand over fist. Because you know what? When they go down, they actually get cheaper.\nPhoto by Anna Nekrashevich on Pexels\nUnitedHealth Group Incorporated (NYSE:UNH) provides health care services, insurance plans, pharmacy care, and data-driven solutions.\nWhile we acknowledge the potential of UNH as an investment, we believe certain AI stocks offer greater upside potential and carry less downside risk. If you're looking for an extremely undervalued AI stock that also stands to benefit significantly from Trump-era tariffs and the onshoring trend, see our free report on the best short-term AI stock .\nREAD NEXT: 33 Stocks That Should Double in 3 Years and 15 Stocks That Will Make You Rich in 10 Years\nDisclosure: None. Follow Insider Monkey on Google News ."
